diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 99c49a7..281b438 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -7,6 +7,20 @@ All notable changes to the **Kernel Borderlands** project are documented below.
 ## [Unreleased] - 2026-09-14
 
 ### Fixed
+- **`kbopd` crashed for real on this dev host after the user's own redesign turned `kb-tui` into a unified binary that also serves the dashboard** (`kb-op/kb-tui/src/main.rs`, `Makefile`, `packaging/systemd/kbopd.service`): `kb-op/kb-tui` was extended (axum + rust-embed, `src/dash.rs`) so the same compiled binary, installed as `kbopd`, embeds `kb-dashboard`'s production build and serves it over HTTP when run headless (no controlling TTY) — a real, working design, confirmed by building and running it directly. Three concrete, independently-wrong hardcoded paths were causing the actual failure on this machine (`kbopd.service`: `code=exited, status=1/FAILURE`, and running the deployed binary by hand reproduced `Error: Os { code: 6, ... "No such device or address" }`):
+  1. `kbopd.service`'s `ExecStart` pointed at `/opt/kb/bin/kbopd` — a stale manual-test location from an earlier point in the redesign, not where anything actually gets installed (`/usr/local/bin/`, matching every other unit). The live, correctly-installed `/usr/local/bin/kbopd` ran fine when tested directly, isolating the bug to the unit file's path, not the binary.
+  2. `main.rs`'s two `mcp-server` child-process spawns used two *different*, both-wrong hardcoded paths (`/opt/kb/bin/mcp-server` in headless mode, `/opt/bin/mcp-server` — missing `/kb/` — in interactive mode) — neither matches `/usr/local/bin/mcp-server`, where the top-level `Makefile`'s `install` target actually puts it. Fixed both to the real path.
+  3. The `Makefile`'s `install` target built `kbd_sensor` but never installed it — confirmed on disk: every other binary in `/usr/local/bin/` was from the most recent `make install` run except `kbd_sensor`, which was a build old. Added the missing `install -Dm755 kb-core/build/kbd_sensor /usr/local/bin/kbd_sensor` line.
+  Also dropped `kbopd.service`'s `--config /etc/kb/config.yaml` flag and matching `ReadPaths=` — `kbopd` (the kb-tui binary) parses no CLI arguments at all today, so it was silently ignored dead weight, not a real, working flag. Verified: `make build` clean, ran the freshly-built `bin/kbopd` headless (`< /dev/null`, no TTY) — embedded dashboard actually serves real HTTP 200 on `127.0.0.1:8082`, and `mcp-server` spawns as a genuine child process from the corrected path (confirmed via `ps -ef` showing the real parent/child PID relationship). Deploying this fix to the live system still requires `sudo` (not run automatically) — re-run `packaging/install.sh` or `sudo make install` + `sudo cp packaging/systemd/kbopd.service /etc/systemd/system/` + `sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl restart kbopd`. [Claude Code]
+
+### Added
+- **`packaging/install.sh` — single entry point running every step from `packaging/README.md`'s Installing section** (build, `sudo make install`, `sudo scripts/setup/provision.sh`, systemd unit + SSH config install, `daemon-reload`, `enable --now` the 4 real units): requested directly, so a real-host test doesn't require re-typing 7 manual commands. Deliberately runs the build step as the invoking user (not under `sudo`) so Go/Rust use that user's own toolchain caches, and shells out to `sudo` individually for each step that actually needs root, rather than requiring the whole script to run as root. `kbagents.service`/`kbopd.service` intentionally left unstarted (no real binaries). Also updated `packaging/README.md`'s stale `kb-checker.service` dependency description (still said `Requires=kb-sensor.service`, corrected below) and added a note pointing at `docs/architecture/enabling-bpf-lsm.md` — everything verified this session ran inside a container sharing this sandbox's kernel, which proves the systemd wiring is correct but not that a different target kernel has LSM/BPF enabled.
+
+### Fixed
+- **`kb-checker.service`'s own `Requires=kb-sensor.service` caused kb-checker to kill itself mid-containment-response, turning every normal recovery action into a false full Lockdown** (`packaging/systemd/kb-checker.service`): this was a real bug introduced earlier in this same session, not pre-existing — `Requires=` was added to fix a genuine ordering gap (kb-checker auditing a sensor that hadn't loaded yet), but `Requires=` also propagates unit *stops* bidirectionally: whenever the required unit stops, systemd stops the requiring unit too, regardless of who initiated the stop. Since `kb-checker`'s own recovery logic (`report/mod.rs`'s `trigger_auto_recovery`) routinely calls `systemctl stop/restart kb-sensor` as a normal part of its job, every one of those calls immediately made systemd also start stopping `kb-checker.service` itself — sending SIGTERM to its entire cgroup, killing both the main `kb-checker` process and its still-running `systemctl stop kb-sensor` child at the same instant. The child dying to that externally-imposed SIGTERM was then misread by `report/mod.rs` as "the stop failed" (`Ok(s) if s.success()` false because the child was killed by a signal, not because it exited non-zero on its own), which triggered the full `pkill -9`/eBPF-detach/`iptables`-quarantine Lockdown fallback — from a safety daemon that was already mid-shutdown. Confirmed via three consecutive real boot tests, each isolating one more variable, ending with full unfiltered `journalctl` output showing `systemd[1]: Stopping kb-checker.service` land in the exact half-second between `kb-checker`'s own "Invoking systemctl stop kb-sensor" and "Sensor unload failed" log lines. Fixed by changing `kb-checker.service`'s `kb-sensor.service` dependency from `Requires=` to `Wants=` (kept `After=` for both, and kept `Requires=kbd.service` as-is — kb-checker never stops kbd itself, so that propagation direction is fine). Reverified: same real liveness-check failure now completes as "Sensor unload triggered successfully," zero Lockdown, `kb-checker.service` `NRestarts=0` across a 70s+ run through multiple genuine recovery cycles.
+- **kb-checker's Ray/AADS-swarm-unreachable check restarted a perfectly healthy `kbd_sensor` for no reason, and could cascade into full Lockdown** (`kb-checker/src/main.rs`): per explicit direction, downgraded — AADS/Ray being unreachable is a downstream-infrastructure-readiness problem, not evidence of kernel/eBPF tampering, so restarting the sensor over it has no causal justification and risked colliding with another task's concurrent recovery action (the exact mechanism behind the original all-tasks-fail-at-once Lockdown seen before the grace-period fix, below). Task 3's loop no longer calls `maybe_trigger_recovery`/`trigger_auto_recovery` at all — it still records the failure via `update_state` (so `/api/services` and the loop's own health status correctly reflect it), it just no longer touches `kbd_sensor`. Verified: `cargo build`/`cargo test` clean (2/2).
+- **kb-checker had zero startup grace period — every single cold boot self-triggered the full Tampering Containment Lockdown (kill sensor, detach eBPF, iptables-quarantine the host), even with nothing actually wrong** (`kb-checker/src/main.rs`): found via the same real container boot test as the fixes below — with `kbd`'s `sd_notify` fix and `StateDirectory=kbd` in place, all three units (`kbd`/`kb-sensor`/`kb-checker`) genuinely reached `active (running)`, but `kb-checker`'s 6 audit tasks each run their first check at T+0 with no warm-up, and a single failure on ANY of them (even a fully expected transient one — the containment map not pinned yet a few hundred ms after the sensor reports hooks attached, or the AADS/Ray swarm simply not up yet) immediately escalated to the same Lockdown response reserved for genuine tampering. This happened on every boot, unconditionally. Added `STARTUP_GRACE_PERIOD` (15s, in-memory `Instant` comparison only — no persistent state, no network, consistent with `kb-checker`'s KISS/no-state/no-network design invariant) via a new `maybe_trigger_recovery` wrapper around all 6 call sites of `trigger_auto_recovery`; the underlying check still runs and still logs failures during the grace window, only the escalation is suppressed.
+- **`kb-checker`'s gRPC health check against `kbd` queried the wrong service name — permanently, not just at boot** (`kb-checker/src/service_check/mod.rs`): found immediately after the grace-period fix above, when a real `NotFound: unknown service` kept firing on every check cycle even well past the 15s grace window. `kbd` registers its `grpc_health_v1` service under the plain string `"kernel-borderlands"` (`kb-control-plane/internal/controlplane/controlplane.go`'s `ServiceName` const, asserted against in 8+ of that package's own tests) — `kb-checker` was querying `"kb.KernelBorderlands"` instead, which matches nothing kbd ever registers. Fixed by querying the real name (new `CONTROL_PLANE_SERVICE_NAME` const). Also fixed the test suite that failed to catch this: `checker_tests.rs`'s `MockHealthService` previously ignored the requested service name entirely and always returned SERVING, so it could never have caught a name mismatch — made it check-name-aware (returns real `grpc_health_v1` `NotFound` semantics for an unregistered name, mirroring `kbd`'s actual behavior) and added `test_grpc_health_check_uses_kbd_real_service_name`, which pins the exact string so this can't silently drift again. `cargo test` passes (2/2, was 1/1). Reverified in the same container: `kb-checker`'s gRPC check now logs `SERVING` continuously past the grace period, with zero restarts, until a *genuinely* absent dependency (Ray/AADS, not running in this bare 3-unit test) correctly triggers a real, accurate restart cycle — not a false positive.
 - **`docs/architecture/boot_sequence_spec.md` still described `kbd` as the thing that loads `kbd_sensor`'s eBPF hooks, in three separate places, with the ordering backwards** (the mermaid sequence diagram, Phase 1's numbered list, the `T+5.00s`/`T+5.50s` chronological timeline, and the "eBPF Hook Loading Failure" fail-safe scenario in §4): an earlier pass this session added `kb-sensor.service` and a pointer note ahead of §3's unit-file listing, but never went back to correct the doc's own narrative text, which still said `kbd` itself compiles/loads/attaches the sensor's hooks — and worse, placed that step *before* `kbd`'s readiness notification, which is backwards from the real systemd chain (`kb-sensor.service`'s `Requires=kbd.service` means it cannot even attempt to start until after `kbd` reports ready). Corrected all four spots: added a `Sensor` participant to the mermaid diagram and moved its lane after `kbd`'s readiness notification; swapped Phase 1's step 3/4 order and reassigned eBPF loading to `kbd_sensor`; swapped the `T+5.00s`/`T+5.50s` timeline entries the same way; rewrote the eBPF-failure scenario in §4 to describe the real mechanism (a `kb-sensor.service` crash-loop blocks `kb-checker.service` via its `Requires=`, not a `kbd.service` readiness failure — `kbd` itself is unaffected). Also updated §2 Phase 2 step 1 to mention `kb-checker.service`'s `Requires=kb-sensor.service` alongside `kbd.service`, which the same earlier pass added to the unit file but never reflected in the narrative. [Claude Code]
 - **kbd never sent `sd_notify(READY=1)` despite `kbd.service` declaring `Type=notify` — the entire boot dependency chain silently never proceeded** (`kb-control-plane/cmd/kbd/main.go`, `go.mod`): found by actually booting `packaging/systemd/*.service` in a privileged systemd-enabled Docker container (`docker run --privileged --cgroupns=host -v /sys/fs/cgroup:/sys/fs/cgroup:rw ...`) instead of only running `systemd-analyze verify` — that tool only checks unit-file syntax, it never runs `ExecStart`, so it couldn't have caught this. Confirmed via `journalctl -b`: `kbd.service` sat forever in `activating (start)`, and `kb-checker.service`/`kb-sensor.service` both correctly refused to start with "Dependency failed" (their `Requires=kbd.service` was working exactly as designed — it just never resolved because `kbd` never told systemd it was ready). Root cause: zero `sd_notify` calls anywhere in `kb-control-plane` (confirmed via grep before writing any fix). Added `github.com/coreos/go-systemd/v22`'s `daemon.SdNotify`, called from a new `notifySystemdReady()` in `main.go` after `cp.Start()` returns — implements exactly what `docs/architecture/boot_sequence_spec.md` §2 Phase 1 step 4 already claimed happened ("kbd verifies its local sockets are listening and sends a ready signal back to systemd"), which was previously just narrative, not code. Polls for `kbd.sock`/`kbct.sock`'s existence (bounded, 3s) before notifying, since `internal/ipc`'s two listeners bind inside their own goroutines and aren't synchronously confirmed by the time `cp.Start()` returns — a real, still-open minor race, left as-is rather than refactoring `Listener`'s API under this change; `SdNotify` is a no-op when `NOTIFY_SOCKET` isn't set, so this is safe for every non-systemd dev/manual run too. Reverified in the same container after the fix: `kbd.service` now reaches genuine `active (running)`.
 - **`kbd.service`'s real `--db /var/lib/kbd/state.db` path crash-looped forever — the directory never gets created by anything** (`packaging/systemd/kbd.service`): also found via the same real boot test, before the `sd_notify` fix was even in place — `journalctl` showed `unable to open database file: no such file or directory`, repeating every `RestartSec=3`. Fixed with `StateDirectory=kbd`, the systemd-native equivalent of the same unit's existing `RuntimeDirectory=kb` for `/run/kb`, rather than adding a manual `mkdir` to `scripts/setup/provision.sh` (ties the directory's lifecycle to the unit itself, the idiomatic systemd answer to exactly this).
